"helpText" = "## Basics\n1. Click “Add App…” and choose an app. You can also drag an .app onto the list.\n2. Click the shortcut button in its row (“None”) and press the keys to register. Esc cancels.\n3. Press the shortcut to launch the app or bring it to the front.\n- Pressing the shortcut of the app already in front does nothing.\n- The result appears at the bottom of the main window and at the top of the menu-bar menu. Failures are also shown near the top of the screen.\n\n## Per-app options (… menu in each row)\n- **Bring all windows to front**: brings every window of the app forward (on by default).\n- **Reopen a window if there is none**: when the app is running without windows, asks it to open one, like clicking its Dock icon.\n- **Hide other apps**: hides the other apps after switching.\n- **Switch input source to “ABC” after launching**: selects the ABC input source after switching, even when the app was already in front. Fails with a message if ABC is not among your input sources.\n- **Run on double tap**: runs when the key is pressed twice in a row. One key can have a single-press row and a double-tap row. For such keys the single press waits about 0.3 s for a second press, so it runs slightly later.\n- **Choose Item to Open…**: after launching, opens the chosen folder or file with the app (for example, a shared folder in Path Finder).\n- **Front App Condition**: with “Not in these apps”, the key is not registered while one of the chosen apps is in front, so it works as that app's own shortcut (for example, ⌃⌥I is not used in Chrome). “Only in these apps” is the opposite. Rows with different conditions can share a key; of the rows usable at that moment, the one added first wins.\n- **URL Condition…**: while a browser (Chrome, Safari, Firefox and others) shows a page starting with a listed URL, the key is passed to the browser (for example, keep ⌃⌥I for Google Sheets: https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/). The URL is checked when the key is pressed. Needs Accessibility permission; without it the app launches as usual.\n- **Which Version**: for apps such as InDesign 2025 and 2026 that share a Bundle ID. “Standard” lets macOS choose. “Prefer the newest” uses the highest version among the copies macOS knows and those in folders next to the registered app's folder (for example /Applications/Adobe InDesign 2026). “The app at …” always uses the app picked with “Choose App to Use…”. Except in Standard, the chosen copy is used even if another version is running, and the row shows the version in use.\n- “Launch Now”, “Show in Finder” and “Remove from List” are in this menu too. Right-click a row for the same menu.\n- Double-click an icon or app name to launch it right away without the shortcut (works even if the row is off or hotkeys are paused).\n- Use the search field above the list to filter by app name, Bundle ID or shortcut (for example ⌃⌥I or I). Separate words with spaces to show only rows containing all of them.\n- “Name” and “Shortcut” change the display order. Click the selected one again to switch between ascending and descending.\n- Turn off the checkbox on the left to stop only that row's shortcut.\n\n## Keyboard view\n- Switch with “List / Keyboard” at the top. Registered apps appear on a keyboard as icons; point at a key to see the app name.\n- Choose a modifier set such as ⌃⌥ to show only its apps. “All” shows every set together, colored by set.\n- Click a key to launch, right-click for the same menu as the list. With a modifier set chosen, click an empty key to assign an app to it. Drag an icon to another key to move it, or to swap with an assigned key (tap count and conditions stay; ⌘Z undoes; not in “All”).\n- Hatched keys are macOS shortcuts. ×2 marks double taps, the funnel marks rows with a condition.\n- The layout follows the connected keyboard (switch US / JIS with “Layout”).\n\n## Sharing settings\n- In Settings > Sharing, share your apps with their hotkeys and options, whether hotkeys are on, the Path Finder preference, and the list view and sort order with your other Macs.\n- **Export & Import**: “Export…” writes DocklessLauncher-Settings.plist; on another Mac, choose that file with “Import…”, then Add (keep what is registered and add only missing apps) or Replace (use the file’s settings). Cancel changes nothing. .docklesslauncher (and older .applauncher) files exported by earlier versions can be imported too.\n- **Settings Sync**: click “Set Sync Folder…” and choose a folder in iCloud Drive, Dropbox or similar. Choose the same folder on your other Macs to keep changes in step automatically. With a sync service, keep this folder downloaded and available offline.\n- Apps missing on another Mac show as not found. They stay in the list.\n- Login launch, background, menu bar, Accessibility, window positions and widths, keyboard layout and excluded apps stay per Mac.\n\n## Settings\n- Startup & Background: launch at login (off by default), hide main window at startup, keep running after closing, show in menu bar.\n- Hotkeys: turn all shortcuts on or off, pause until relaunch, prefer Path Finder over Finder while it is running (on by default).\n- Excluded Apps: no shortcut is registered while these apps are in front.\n- Sharing: export and import settings, and sync them through a folder in iCloud Drive, Dropbox or similar.\n- About: version and links.\n- Open Settings with ⌘, and close it with ⌘W.\n\n## Shortcuts\n- A key that is also a macOS shortcut (Screenshot and others in System Settings > Keyboard > Keyboard Shortcuts) is flagged when recorded and in the list. It can still be registered, but macOS may take it first.\n- An app shortcut needs ⌘, ⌥ or ⌃. Standard keys such as ⌘C or ⌘V cannot be used.\n- If several apps share a shortcut (with the same single or double tap), only the one added first is active (the display order does not matter). Shortcuts that could not be registered are marked in their rows.\n- Not every conflict with other apps can be detected. If a shortcut does not respond, choose another or find its owner with HotkeyHound.\n- To open DocklessLauncher's own window with a key, add DocklessLauncher to the list.\n\n## Permissions\nLaunching and switching apps needs no permission (standard macOS APIs, NSWorkspace). Accessibility is used only by the URL condition, to read the browser's URL and pass the key to it. Check its state and open System Settings in Settings > Startup & Background.\n\n## Background and quitting\n- The menu-bar icon offers the main window, Settings, Pause, Help and Quit. Hide the icon with “Menu Bar Settings…” in the app menu (the app keeps running).\n- Closing the window keeps the app and its shortcuts running. Opening the app again from the Dock or Finder shows the main window.\n- Quit with “Quit DocklessLauncher” (app menu, menu bar) or ⌘Q while the app is in front. Shortcuts stop when the app quits.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n- Nothing happens: check that the row is on, hotkeys are enabled, the app is not paused, and no excluded app is in front.\n- “App not found”: the app may have been moved, deleted or renamed. Remove it from the list and add it again.\n- The app does not come forward: if macOS refuses the switch, DocklessLauncher retries automatically through Launch Services.\n\n## Privacy\nNo network access (update checks stay off until a feed is configured). Nothing you do is logged.\n\n## Uninstall\nDelete DocklessLauncher from the Applications folder. To remove its settings too, delete ~/Library/Preferences/jp.dtp-transit.applauncher.plist and remove it from Login Items.";
